WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
Starbucks. She's getting great for all of her age.

00:04.900 --> 00:08.100
Yeah, she's been having a lot of trouble with Arthur lately.

00:08.800 --> 00:10.400
Yeah, like what kind of trouble?

00:12.200 --> 00:15.500
You know, I don't know what kind of trouble.

00:16.700 --> 00:19.000
Oh, I really ought to say anything.

00:19.000 --> 00:20.800
Well, you can tell me I mean definitely.

00:20.800 --> 00:28.800
Well, she's looking this way.

00:28.800 --> 00:33.400
She went in the drugstore.

00:33.400 --> 00:33.800
Go ahead.

00:35.600 --> 00:38.400
He wants the whole family to move out of town.

00:38.400 --> 00:40.200
For Pete's sake, why?

00:40.200 --> 00:43.500
He claims people talk about other people too much.

00:43.500 --> 00:46.000
Where do you ever get an idea like that?

00:46.000 --> 00:46.800
Beats me.

00:46.800 --> 00:51.800
Don't burn that kid.

00:51.800 --> 00:53.600
They know they're not allowed to ride bikes on the sidewalk.

00:53.600 --> 00:58.000
There, there, Miss Rodenbach, we'll give you a hand.

00:58.000 --> 01:00.600
There may well be a law against riding on the sidewalk.

01:00.600 --> 01:01.200
There is.

01:01.200 --> 01:03.800
Did you see who the ball was on the bicycle?

01:03.800 --> 01:04.400
No, I didn't.

01:04.400 --> 01:05.800
It all happened so fast.

01:05.800 --> 01:07.800
Edith Bressing just left.

01:07.800 --> 01:09.000
We were talking about Mrs.

01:09.000 --> 01:09.600
Tarbox.

01:09.600 --> 01:11.600
Why hasn't she gotten gray?

01:11.600 --> 01:12.800
Well, you know why, don't you?

01:12.800 --> 01:13.400
Barn.

01:13.400 --> 01:19.600
Did you see how old the boy was?

01:19.600 --> 01:22.600
No, it was just whoosh and he was gone.

01:22.600 --> 01:25.600
Now, if it happens again, try to remember the following things.

01:25.600 --> 01:32.200
Age of the boy, color of his hair, new or old bicycle, whether or not he was wearing glasses, things like that.

01:32.200 --> 01:33.400
Well, I'll certainly try.

01:33.400 --> 01:34.600
We'd appreciate it.

01:34.600 --> 01:41.000
Of course, a really sound safety procedure would be to give each and every citizen in this town a course in police identification.

01:41.000 --> 01:45.000
But, you know, us trained professionals, we have photographic mind.

01:45.000 --> 01:50.200
Well, I'm certainly glad you were here.

01:50.200 --> 01:52.000
Now you know who it was.

01:52.000 --> 01:54.000
Well, girl, it all happened so fast.

01:54.000 --> 01:55.800
Just whoosh and he was gone.

01:55.800 --> 02:11.400
Hi, Taylor.

02:11.400 --> 02:12.200
Hi, Arnie.

02:12.200 --> 02:15.200
Hey, you got your new Intercontinental Flyer, huh?

02:15.200 --> 02:15.800
Uh-huh.

02:15.800 --> 02:16.600
Yesterday.

02:16.600 --> 02:18.000
Boy, it's a beauty.

02:18.000 --> 02:19.200
Wow.

02:19.200 --> 02:20.800
Is this real chrome?

02:20.800 --> 02:22.800
There's an M in there, Taylor.

02:22.800 --> 02:24.200
I know, chronium.

02:24.200 --> 02:26.000
How much did it cost?

02:26.000 --> 02:27.000
Seventy dollars.

02:27.000 --> 02:28.800
Seventy dollars?

02:28.800 --> 02:32.200
Gosh, you must have been saving up for it since you was a kid.

02:32.200 --> 02:33.800
My dad bought it for me.

02:33.800 --> 02:35.400
For your birthday or something?

02:35.400 --> 02:36.400
Oh, no.

02:36.400 --> 02:38.400
I saved my birthday for something big.

02:38.400 --> 02:40.600
What's bigger than an Intercontinental?

02:40.600 --> 02:41.800
Giants and stuff.

02:41.800 --> 02:45.000
A pony, a boat, all kinds of stuff.

02:45.000 --> 02:46.000
Come on, Taylor.

02:46.000 --> 02:47.600
Hop on the rack and I'll give you a ride.

02:47.600 --> 02:48.400
I can't.

02:48.400 --> 02:50.200
I got to get this garage cleaned up.

02:50.200 --> 02:51.200
Who says?

02:51.200 --> 02:52.000
My pa.

02:52.000 --> 02:53.400
What happens if you don't?

02:53.400 --> 02:55.000
I don't get my quarter this week.

02:55.000 --> 02:57.400
A quarter?

02:57.400 --> 02:59.600
For a job like this?

02:59.600 --> 03:00.600
Oh, no.

03:00.600 --> 03:05.200
Besides this, I take out the ashes, keep the wood box filled, and set the table every night.

03:05.200 --> 03:06.200
Oh, boy.

03:06.200 --> 03:08.400
Did your old man see you coming?

03:08.400 --> 03:09.400
What do you mean?

03:09.400 --> 03:11.800
He's taking advantage of you.

03:11.800 --> 03:13.400
I don't know what you're talking about.

03:13.400 --> 03:14.600
They owe it to you.

03:14.600 --> 03:17.200
You're not supposed to work for your allowance.

03:17.200 --> 03:19.200
What do you think allowance means?

03:19.200 --> 03:20.000
I don't know.

03:20.000 --> 03:23.200
It means money the kid is allowed to have.

03:23.200 --> 03:24.000
Oh?

03:24.000 --> 03:25.600
And without working for it.

03:25.600 --> 03:27.000
It's for being a kid.

03:27.000 --> 03:28.200
Are you sure?

03:28.200 --> 03:30.800
Seems to me my pa already told me if that's so.

03:30.800 --> 03:33.000
They figure what they don't tell you won't hurt you.

03:33.000 --> 03:34.000
Not my pa.

03:34.000 --> 03:35.800
He tells me everything straight.

03:35.800 --> 03:38.800
Okay, so I'm wrong.

03:38.800 --> 03:41.000
But who's cleaning the garage for a quarter?

03:41.000 --> 03:44.000
And who's riding a $70 bike?

03:44.000 --> 03:46.600
Wait a minute, Arnold.

03:46.600 --> 03:49.400
What do you mean?

03:49.400 --> 03:51.000
I told you.

03:51.000 --> 03:54.600
Kids aren't supposed to work for their allowance.

03:54.600 --> 03:56.400
My pa is awful busy.

03:56.400 --> 03:59.400
Maybe he hasn't heard this new stuff.

03:59.400 --> 04:00.800
Maybe.

04:00.800 --> 04:03.800
If I was you, I'd just come right out and tell him.

04:03.800 --> 04:05.600
Okay, I'll ask him.

04:05.600 --> 04:08.000
My pa is real good about things like this.

04:08.000 --> 04:11.800
He'll sit right down with me and we'll talk the whole thing right out in the open.

04:11.800 --> 04:31.800
Boy, you do everything the hard way.

04:31.800 --> 04:33.400
Morning posters came in, Andy.

04:33.400 --> 04:34.200
Oh, good.

04:34.200 --> 04:35.200
File them, will you, Barney?

04:35.200 --> 04:38.000
Yeah.

04:38.000 --> 04:42.000
Let's see what we got here.

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
Henry Shopping Bag Leonetti.

04:45.000 --> 04:48.600
Grand Larceny, $4,000 reward.

04:48.600 --> 04:52.000
Max the Tongue Rasmussen, Grand Theft Auto.

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
Reward, $1,500.

04:55.000 --> 05:00.000
Benjamin Schuster, alias Benji Shoes, alias Benny Shoot.

05:00.000 --> 05:01.200
Forgery.

05:01.200 --> 05:05.600
Reward, $3,000.

05:05.600 --> 05:07.400
You know what this means, Ange?

05:07.400 --> 05:08.200
What?

05:08.200 --> 05:12.400
8,500 American silver cartwheels right in our hands.

05:12.400 --> 05:14.400
Only one small hitch.

05:14.400 --> 05:15.200
We don't know where they are.

05:15.200 --> 05:18.200
Right.

05:18.200 --> 05:18.800
Hi, Pa.

05:18.800 --> 05:19.200
Hello.

05:19.200 --> 05:20.400
Hello.

05:20.400 --> 05:22.200
Hi, Barney.

05:22.200 --> 05:24.200
I thought you were supposed to be cleaning out the garage.

05:24.200 --> 05:26.000
I wanted to have a talk with you.

05:26.000 --> 05:28.800
Must be pretty important for you to break off the middle of the show.

05:28.800 --> 05:31.400
It's preying on my mind, Pa.

05:31.400 --> 05:42.800
And you did right to come here, didn't you?

05:42.800 --> 05:43.400
OK.

05:43.400 --> 05:44.400
Ready?

05:44.400 --> 05:45.400
Ready.

05:45.400 --> 05:46.400
This.

05:46.400 --> 05:52.000
Are there rules for how Pa should treat his son if he's a kid?

05:52.000 --> 05:57.800
Well, of all the questions to come up with, that one will take the cake.

05:57.800 --> 06:02.200
Barney, I hope you and I usually keep these little talks to ourselves.

06:02.200 --> 06:03.800
Oh, sure.

06:03.800 --> 06:06.200
Oh, I understand that.

06:06.200 --> 06:12.400
Oh, I got plenty to do in here anyway.

06:12.400 --> 06:14.400
Are Barney's feelings hurt, Pa?

06:14.400 --> 06:15.600
Oh, I don't think so.

06:15.600 --> 06:19.400
I think I can speak to my own feelings, Andy.

06:19.400 --> 06:20.800
I already asked that of him.

06:20.800 --> 06:21.800
I don't know.

06:21.800 --> 06:23.600
I heard you were kind of sensitive.

06:23.600 --> 06:25.000
Sensitive?

06:25.000 --> 06:26.800
Me, sensitive?

06:26.800 --> 06:28.800
I'm not sensitive, Barney.

06:28.800 --> 06:31.800
Oh, that's a nice thing to get started around.

06:31.800 --> 06:35.400
See, the thing that upsets me is having people say I'm sensitive.

06:35.400 --> 06:38.200
There's one thing I'm not sensitive.

06:38.200 --> 06:41.000
Talk about being misunderstood.

06:41.000 --> 06:41.800
You're a little flushed.

06:41.800 --> 06:42.800
That's spirit.

06:42.800 --> 06:44.800
I'm a very spirited person.

06:44.800 --> 06:47.000
It's a big difference, you know.

06:47.000 --> 06:48.800
Go on and have your little talk.

06:48.800 --> 06:53.200
Don't mean nothing to me anyways.

06:53.200 --> 06:55.200
I'm sorry I hurt Barney's feelings, Pa.

06:55.200 --> 06:56.400
He's very spirited.

06:56.400 --> 06:57.400
Sensitive?

06:57.400 --> 07:00.400
Kind of.

07:00.400 --> 07:03.400
What's this about rules for pa's and son's?

07:03.400 --> 07:05.000
This first.

07:05.000 --> 07:07.600
The 75 cent rule.

07:07.600 --> 07:08.800
The 75 cent rule.

07:08.800 --> 07:12.600
I don't believe I ever heard of that one.

07:12.600 --> 07:15.600
That's what I figured.

07:15.600 --> 07:22.800
What it is, Pa, is that nowadays kids get 75 cents a week allowance instead of 25 cents.

07:22.800 --> 07:25.000
75 cents?

07:25.000 --> 07:26.400
That's a lot of money.

07:26.400 --> 07:31.400
And in a year's time, that'd come to, say, 52 weeks in a year.

07:31.400 --> 07:35.800
It comes to around $40 a year.

07:35.800 --> 07:38.200
That's an awful lot of money for a young'un.

07:38.200 --> 07:39.800
They get it, Pa.

07:39.800 --> 07:41.000
They do, huh?

07:41.000 --> 07:44.200
And they don't have to work for it like I do.

07:44.200 --> 07:46.600
Who is this they you keep talking about?

07:46.600 --> 07:49.400
Oh, Arnold Winkler and everybody.

07:49.400 --> 07:51.400
Arnold Winkler.

07:51.400 --> 07:53.600
I don't believe I know him, do I?

07:53.600 --> 07:55.200
You're new from Raleigh.

07:55.200 --> 07:56.000
Oh, I see.

07:56.000 --> 07:59.600
And the Raleigh rules say 75 cents and no work, huh?

07:59.600 --> 08:04.000
I guessed.

08:04.000 --> 08:05.200
You want it straight, don't you?

08:05.200 --> 08:06.600
Uh-huh.

08:06.600 --> 08:08.400
Okay, here it goes.

08:08.400 --> 08:12.200
There are no rules for pa's and son's.

08:12.200 --> 08:13.200
It's as simple as this.

08:13.200 --> 08:20.600
Each mother or father raises his boy or girl, as the case may be, the way that he thinks is best.

08:20.600 --> 08:25.000
And I think it's best for you to give a quarter and work for it.

08:25.000 --> 08:31.800
You see, when you give something, in this instance, clean the garage, and you get something in return, like a quarter,

08:31.800 --> 08:34.600
well, that's the greatest feeling in the world.

08:34.600 --> 08:36.800
You do feel good after working, don't you?

08:36.800 --> 08:37.400
Uh-huh.

08:37.400 --> 08:39.400
Good and tired.

08:39.400 --> 08:45.000
Well, as you get bigger, well, you'll be doing more and more work for more and more return.

08:45.000 --> 08:48.200
And that good feeling will get bigger.

08:48.200 --> 08:49.600
Do you understand what I mean?

08:49.600 --> 08:50.600
I think so.

08:50.600 --> 08:51.600
Good.

08:51.600 --> 08:55.000
I'm not going to get the 75 cents.

08:55.000 --> 08:57.200
And I have to work for the 25.

08:57.200 --> 08:59.000
Right.

08:59.000 --> 09:00.000
It's all clear to you?

09:00.000 --> 09:00.600
Yeah.

09:00.600 --> 09:04.600
The bigger you get, the tireder you get.

09:04.600 --> 09:07.800
Well, you just think about that for a while.

09:07.800 --> 09:09.200
Do I have to?

09:09.200 --> 09:10.600
Don't you want to think about it?

09:10.600 --> 09:13.600
It makes me kind of sad.

09:13.600 --> 09:17.200
Well, the thing to do when you're feeling sad is to shoot for the good feeling.

09:17.200 --> 09:18.600
Clean the garage.

09:18.600 --> 09:19.400
Right.

09:19.400 --> 09:20.200
So long, Paul.

09:20.200 --> 09:23.600
So long.

09:23.600 --> 09:25.000
So long, Barney.

09:25.000 --> 09:26.000
So long, Hope.

09:26.000 --> 09:33.200
And that comes to $39 exactly.

09:33.200 --> 09:34.600
Kids.

09:34.600 --> 09:37.400
What do they know about life, huh?

09:37.400 --> 09:40.800
Well, I'll tell you one thing.

09:40.800 --> 09:43.400
A whole lot more than we give them credit for.

09:43.400 --> 09:46.200
Yeah, that's true.

09:46.200 --> 09:48.000
But they need guidance.

09:48.000 --> 09:49.000
Yeah, that's true.

09:49.000 --> 09:55.800
And this time you really muffed it.

09:55.800 --> 09:57.600
How's that?

09:57.600 --> 10:02.800
Well, not being emotionally involved with the child, I think I can be pretty doggone objective.

10:02.800 --> 10:04.800
Oh.

10:04.800 --> 10:08.800
You've been at the magazine section of the Sunday paper again, ain't you?

10:08.800 --> 10:11.000
I skimmed through it.

10:11.000 --> 10:14.400
And on the subject of child rearing, it said?

10:14.400 --> 10:16.600
There's a definite trend towards stronger discipline.

10:16.600 --> 10:17.200
Like what?

10:17.200 --> 10:19.000
Like a good clout once in a while.

10:19.000 --> 10:24.200
Andy, have you ever known the magazine section of the Sunday paper to lie?

10:24.200 --> 10:26.000
Did your father ever hit you?

10:26.000 --> 10:26.600
Well, he couldn't.

10:26.600 --> 10:29.600
I was a lot bigger than he was.

10:29.600 --> 10:31.200
I thought as a child you were sickly.

10:31.200 --> 10:33.200
Well, he was sicklier.

10:33.200 --> 10:35.600
Now, Andy, the way I talk to a son of mine...

10:35.600 --> 10:36.200
Wait a minute.

10:36.200 --> 10:36.800
Wait a minute.

10:36.800 --> 10:37.400
Wait a minute.

10:37.400 --> 10:38.400
Wait a minute.

10:38.400 --> 10:41.200
Just don't start off on that imaginary child of yours again.

10:41.200 --> 10:42.200
Look, Andy, I'm only trying to...

10:42.200 --> 10:46.200
All the theories in the world don't mean a thing until you got a child of your own.

10:46.200 --> 10:50.800
There ain't no use talking about it because you don't know what you're going to do until you're faced with it.

10:50.800 --> 10:54.600
What are you driving at?

10:54.600 --> 10:56.400
Just think about it.

10:56.400 --> 10:59.200
And when it clears up in your mind, do me a favor.

10:59.200 --> 11:00.200
What's that?

11:00.200 --> 11:07.800
Don't be sensitive about it.

11:07.800 --> 11:10.400
And he said, well, when you're sad, shoot for a good feeling.

11:10.400 --> 11:11.600
I said, clean the garage.

11:11.600 --> 11:13.800
And he said, right, and here I am.

11:13.800 --> 11:15.800
I knew he'd fool that one, Taylor.

11:15.800 --> 11:16.800
How?

11:16.800 --> 11:18.400
Because they're all the same.

11:18.400 --> 11:23.400
Taylor, I've never known a kid yet who could win in a man-to-man talk.

11:23.400 --> 11:25.000
I don't know about that.

11:25.000 --> 11:28.400
My pa and I, we decide a lot of things talking that way.

11:28.400 --> 11:30.000
He's very good at explaining.

11:30.000 --> 11:30.800
I know.

11:30.800 --> 11:33.200
At explaining why you're wrong.

11:33.200 --> 11:36.400
But has he ever explained to you why you're right?

11:36.400 --> 11:37.800
Not that I remember.

11:37.800 --> 11:40.000
Look, Taylor, I lived in Raleigh.

11:40.000 --> 11:41.600
I've been around.

11:41.600 --> 11:43.400
Heck, I left home when I was seven.

11:43.400 --> 11:44.200
You did?

11:44.200 --> 11:46.000
Went to summer camp for two weeks.

11:46.000 --> 11:47.200
Think it from me.

11:47.200 --> 11:49.200
Talking is a waste of time.

11:49.200 --> 11:51.400
You have to take action.

11:51.400 --> 11:52.200
Action?

11:52.200 --> 11:53.200
Sure.

11:53.200 --> 11:54.800
What kind of action?

11:54.800 --> 11:56.200
Temper tantrums.

11:56.200 --> 11:57.200
Why?

11:57.200 --> 11:58.200
To scare them.

11:58.200 --> 12:00.000
Shake them up a little bit.

12:00.000 --> 12:03.600
Have you ever held your breath swimming under the raft?

12:03.600 --> 12:04.200
No.

12:04.200 --> 12:07.000
I mean at home, to get something you want.

12:07.000 --> 12:08.600
Sounds kind of spiteful.

12:08.600 --> 12:10.000
Listen, it works.

12:10.000 --> 12:13.200
Your face gets kind of red, and your jaws get tight.

12:13.200 --> 12:15.200
And oh boy, do they get scared.

12:15.200 --> 12:17.600
They think you're going to get seriously sick.

12:17.600 --> 12:19.400
Or maybe even bust a vessel.

12:19.400 --> 12:20.000
Gee.

12:20.000 --> 12:22.000
Oh yeah, there's all kinds of them.

12:22.000 --> 12:25.800
Kicking a table leg, rolling around on the floor,

12:25.800 --> 12:29.800
kicking your feet, pretending you can't stop crying.

12:29.800 --> 12:32.200
Gosh, I don't know.

12:32.200 --> 12:34.600
Oh, for crying out loud, Taylor.

12:34.600 --> 12:37.600
Ha, ha.

12:37.600 --> 12:39.600
Hey, Arnold.

12:39.600 --> 12:41.200
Take it easy, Arnold.

12:41.200 --> 12:43.400
I didn't mean to say nothing wrong.

12:43.400 --> 12:44.400
I'm sorry, Arnold.

12:44.400 --> 12:46.000
I'm sorry.

12:46.000 --> 12:46.800
Get the idea?

12:46.800 --> 12:51.600
Ha, ha.

12:51.600 --> 12:53.200
And it works?

12:53.200 --> 12:54.000
Every time.

12:54.000 --> 12:55.000
Ha, ha.

12:55.000 --> 12:56.000
Ha, ha.

12:56.000 --> 12:57.000
Ha, ha.

12:57.000 --> 12:58.000
Ha, ha.

12:58.000 --> 12:59.000
Ha, ha.

12:59.000 --> 13:00.000
Ha, ha.

13:00.000 --> 13:01.000
Ha, ha.

13:01.000 --> 13:02.000
Ha, ha.

13:02.000 --> 13:03.000
Ha, ha.

13:03.000 --> 13:04.000
Ha, ha.

13:04.000 --> 13:05.000
Ha, ha.

13:05.000 --> 13:06.000
Ha, ha.

13:06.000 --> 13:07.000
Ha, ha.

13:07.000 --> 13:08.000
Ha, ha.

13:08.000 --> 13:09.000
Ha, ha.

13:09.000 --> 13:10.000
Ha, ha.

13:10.000 --> 13:11.000
Ha, ha.

13:11.000 --> 13:14.000
Hey, stop that bike.

13:14.000 --> 13:15.000
What's the matter?

13:15.000 --> 13:18.000
You're not allowed to ride a bike on the sidewalk.

13:18.000 --> 13:19.000
Oh, I'm sorry, sir.

13:19.000 --> 13:20.000
I didn't know that.

13:20.000 --> 13:21.000
Well, now you know.

13:21.000 --> 13:22.600
Bike riding on the streets only.

13:22.600 --> 13:26.000
City statute 249A, section Roman numeral 5.

13:26.000 --> 13:27.000
I won't do it again, sir.

13:27.000 --> 13:28.000
I promise.

13:28.000 --> 13:29.000
All right.

13:29.000 --> 13:30.000
See that you don't.

13:30.000 --> 13:44.000
Oh, watch it.

13:44.000 --> 13:47.000
Dog gone and I warned him and he deliberately rode on the sidewalk again.

13:47.000 --> 13:48.000
Is that true, son?

13:48.000 --> 13:50.000
Did you hear Officer Byte warn you?

13:50.000 --> 13:51.000
Yes, sir.

13:51.000 --> 13:53.000
Why'd you do it again for?

13:53.000 --> 13:55.000
Because I wanted to.

13:55.000 --> 13:57.000
What's your name?

13:57.000 --> 13:58.000
Arnold Winkler.

13:58.000 --> 14:00.000
Arnold Winkler?

14:00.000 --> 14:01.000
Will you let go of my bike?

14:01.000 --> 14:03.000
You have no right to hold my bike like that.

14:03.000 --> 14:06.000
You just simmer down there, young fella.

14:06.000 --> 14:09.000
You're the one that's been riding around on the sidewalk knocking folks over, ain't you?

14:09.000 --> 14:11.000
I'll tell my dad about this.

14:11.000 --> 14:12.000
Then you'll be sorry.

14:12.000 --> 14:14.000
Oh, go ahead and tell him and see if we care.

14:14.000 --> 14:15.000
Nah.

14:15.000 --> 14:16.000
Nah.

14:16.000 --> 14:25.000
Oh, Barn, let's try to keep this on adult level.

14:25.000 --> 14:27.000
Young man, I'm going to have to take this bike and hold it.

14:27.000 --> 14:28.000
You can't take my bike.

14:28.000 --> 14:29.000
It's my bike.

14:29.000 --> 14:30.000
That won't do you any good.

14:30.000 --> 14:32.000
That won't do you any good.

14:32.000 --> 14:33.000
You can't take my bike.

14:33.000 --> 14:34.000
It's my bike.

14:34.000 --> 14:36.000
Now, you listen here, young fella.

14:36.000 --> 14:39.000
When an officer gives you a warning, you're supposed to mind.

14:39.000 --> 14:43.000
But I expect you'll know we're not fooling when we take this bike and keep it for a week or two.

14:43.000 --> 14:44.000
You'll see.

14:44.000 --> 14:45.000
I'll tell my father.

14:45.000 --> 14:46.000
Good, good.

14:46.000 --> 14:48.000
You tell him to come right on down and we'll talk about it.

14:48.000 --> 14:49.000
Yeah.

14:49.000 --> 14:51.000
Now, get on off of there.

14:51.000 --> 14:55.000
Now, go on home.

14:55.000 --> 14:56.000
You'll see.

14:56.000 --> 15:00.000
I'm going to tell my dad.

15:00.000 --> 15:02.000
Talky little devil, ain't he?

15:02.000 --> 15:03.000
Yeah.

15:03.000 --> 15:05.000
Imagine him talking to us like that.

15:05.000 --> 15:06.000
Arrogant little cuss.

15:06.000 --> 15:08.000
Thinks he can make up his own rules.

15:08.000 --> 15:09.000
Yeah.

15:09.000 --> 15:11.000
Well, take the bike and impound it, will you?

15:11.000 --> 15:19.000
Hmm.

15:19.000 --> 15:21.000
What are you doing?

15:21.000 --> 15:32.000
I'm riding on the sidewalk.

15:32.000 --> 15:36.000
How's it coming?

15:36.000 --> 15:37.000
Coming right along.

15:37.000 --> 15:39.000
Got a nice feel to it.

15:39.000 --> 15:41.000
I'll tell you what.

15:41.000 --> 15:47.000
When you're finished with it, tell me what it is and I'll give you 50 cents for it.

15:47.000 --> 15:49.000
Oh, and I put the bike in the shed.

15:49.000 --> 15:50.000
Good.

15:50.000 --> 15:51.000
Hi, Pa.

15:51.000 --> 15:52.000
Oh, hi, son.

15:52.000 --> 15:55.000
Pa, can I talk to you about something I don't understand?

15:55.000 --> 15:56.000
Sure.

15:56.000 --> 16:03.000
Come on in.

16:03.000 --> 16:04.000
Oh, that's nice.

16:04.000 --> 16:07.000
Very nice.

16:07.000 --> 16:09.000
I just don't understand it, Pa.

16:09.000 --> 16:10.000
What?

16:10.000 --> 16:13.000
None of the other guys work for their allowance.

16:13.000 --> 16:15.000
I thought we had this all thrashed out before.

16:15.000 --> 16:16.000
We did.

16:16.000 --> 16:17.000
Why bring it up again?

16:17.000 --> 16:18.000
Cause.

16:18.000 --> 16:19.000
Cause why?

16:19.000 --> 16:21.000
Cause.

16:21.000 --> 16:24.000
Son, that causing can go on and on if you give it its head.

16:24.000 --> 16:26.000
Now, what's this all about?

16:26.000 --> 16:31.000
I just don't want to work for my allowance.

16:31.000 --> 16:34.000
Hope you're not very busy right now.

16:34.000 --> 16:37.000
Like I say, we've been through all that before.

16:37.000 --> 16:41.000
I still don't want to work for my allowance.

16:41.000 --> 16:43.000
Fine.

16:43.000 --> 16:44.000
You don't have to.

16:44.000 --> 16:46.000
Oh, boy!

16:46.000 --> 16:47.000
No work, no allowance.

16:47.000 --> 16:48.000
That's not fair!

16:48.000 --> 16:50.000
Don't raise your voice to me.

16:50.000 --> 16:51.000
Now, you get on out of here.

16:51.000 --> 17:03.000
I got things to do.

17:03.000 --> 17:04.000
What are you doing?

17:04.000 --> 17:07.000
I don't know.

17:07.000 --> 17:10.000
Opie, I asked you what you're doing.

17:10.000 --> 17:13.000
I was holding my breath.

17:13.000 --> 17:22.000
Good lung exercise.

17:22.000 --> 17:25.000
Opie, what are you doing now?

17:25.000 --> 17:27.000
I'm crying and I can't stop.

17:27.000 --> 17:53.000
Shame.

17:53.000 --> 17:56.000
What are you doing now?

17:56.000 --> 18:00.000
I was having a tantrum.

18:00.000 --> 18:12.000
Well, don't get your clothes all dirty.

18:12.000 --> 18:13.000
Sheriff?

18:13.000 --> 18:14.000
Yes, sir. You're the boy's father?

18:14.000 --> 18:15.000
That's right. Simon Winkler.

18:15.000 --> 18:16.000
Andy Taylor.

18:16.000 --> 18:18.000
Now, then, what's this all about?

18:18.000 --> 18:21.000
249A, section Roman numeral 5.

18:21.000 --> 18:27.000
Passenger carrying wheeled vehicle making passage and or transcending area designated for pedestrian traffic only.

18:27.000 --> 18:33.000
Suspect duly warned, blunted said warning, and ignoring same repeated offense within a 24-hour period.

18:33.000 --> 18:36.000
All right, so he rode his bike on the sidewalk.

18:36.000 --> 18:38.000
I was there, Mr. Winkler.

18:38.000 --> 18:42.000
Arnold was given warning and continued to ride his bicycle on the sidewalk.

18:42.000 --> 18:45.000
The offense was clearly defined under normal weather conditions.

18:45.000 --> 18:49.000
There was plenty of room. I wasn't hurting anybody.

18:49.000 --> 18:52.000
People are coming in and out of stores on Main Street every minute.

18:52.000 --> 18:55.000
You never know when somebody's going to walk right out in front of you.

18:55.000 --> 18:57.000
Everybody's against me.

18:57.000 --> 18:58.000
There, there, son.

18:58.000 --> 19:01.000
Can't even have a little fun.

19:01.000 --> 19:02.000
Now you see what you've done?

19:02.000 --> 19:04.000
You got the boy all upset.

19:04.000 --> 19:06.000
Was it such a crime?

19:06.000 --> 19:10.000
Seems to me you people would have more important things to do than go around frightening children.

19:10.000 --> 19:11.000
Now, look here, Mr. Winkler.

19:11.000 --> 19:15.000
Arnold was warned and continued to ride his bicycle on the sidewalk.

19:15.000 --> 19:17.000
Now, we're going to impound that bicycle.

19:17.000 --> 19:19.000
He wants to use it according to regulation.

19:19.000 --> 19:21.000
Oh, for heaven's sake, sir.

19:21.000 --> 19:24.000
Can't you see this is a very sensitive child?

19:24.000 --> 19:26.000
Obviously, you don't understand him.

19:26.000 --> 19:29.000
Arnold's a good boy. You just don't understand him.

19:29.000 --> 19:30.000
Oh, I think I understand him.

19:30.000 --> 19:32.000
He's a good boy, really.

19:32.000 --> 19:35.000
A little high-spirited, perhaps, but weren't we all at his age?

19:35.000 --> 19:37.000
I wasn't.

19:37.000 --> 19:39.000
The law is the law, Mr. Winkler.

19:39.000 --> 19:43.000
Now, if we don't teach children to live in society today, what's going to happen to them when they grow up?

19:43.000 --> 19:44.000
For heaven's sake, sir.

19:44.000 --> 19:47.000
If the boy's not a criminal, I didn't say he was.

19:47.000 --> 19:50.000
Now, what he does at home is none of our business.

19:50.000 --> 19:53.000
But when he gets out on the street, he's going to have to answer to us.

19:53.000 --> 19:55.000
I can show you in the statute book.

19:55.000 --> 19:59.000
The minimum punishment for this offense is impounding the bicycle for one year.

19:59.000 --> 20:02.000
You can't do it. I demand you return that bike and now.

20:02.000 --> 20:04.000
Now, you look here. You're that boy's father.

20:04.000 --> 20:05.000
You're responsible for his actions.

20:05.000 --> 20:08.000
Now, he's too young to be locked up.

20:08.000 --> 20:11.000
But if you're not going to take responsibility, maybe I ought to lock you up.

20:11.000 --> 20:12.000
You ever think of that?

20:12.000 --> 20:15.000
Yeah. Well, my dad ain't even scared of that, are you?

20:15.000 --> 20:17.000
Make them put you in jail.

20:17.000 --> 20:18.000
That'll show them.

20:18.000 --> 20:21.000
Go on, Dad. Show them they can't push me around.

20:21.000 --> 20:24.000
Go on. Put him in jail. He won't care.

20:27.000 --> 20:28.000
How's that?

20:28.000 --> 20:31.000
My dad'll show you. He's tough.

20:32.000 --> 20:33.000
You, uh...

20:34.000 --> 20:36.000
You want me to lock your father up?

20:36.000 --> 20:38.000
He ain't afraid of you.

20:38.000 --> 20:42.000
I don't want to lose my brand-new bike. I just got it.

20:42.000 --> 20:44.000
You'd rather I put your father in jail?

20:44.000 --> 20:46.000
I want my bike!

20:59.000 --> 21:02.000
Sheriff, there won't be any need to impound that bike.

21:02.000 --> 21:03.000
How's that?

21:04.000 --> 21:06.000
I'd like to have it.

21:06.000 --> 21:08.000
I'm gonna sell it.

21:08.000 --> 21:10.000
Sell it? You're gonna sell my bike?

21:10.000 --> 21:11.000
That's right, Arnold.

21:11.000 --> 21:14.000
But it's my bike! You can't sell my bike!

21:14.000 --> 21:15.000
Be quiet, Arnold.

21:17.000 --> 21:19.000
Marty, you won't go get the bicycle.

21:19.000 --> 21:20.000
Ten-four.

21:21.000 --> 21:23.000
No, no, no, no!

21:23.000 --> 21:25.000
I won't let you! You can't do that!

21:25.000 --> 21:27.000
Arnold, be quiet!

21:28.000 --> 21:32.000
Miss Winkler, would you like to continue this father-and-son discussion in quiet?

21:32.000 --> 21:33.000
Huh?

21:33.000 --> 21:38.000
I say, would you like to continue this in quiet? There's a real nice woodshed out back.

21:38.000 --> 21:39.000
Woodshed?

21:39.000 --> 21:40.000
Mm-hmm.

21:41.000 --> 21:44.000
Good old-fashioned woodshed. Real nice one.

21:46.000 --> 21:47.000
Hmm?

21:48.000 --> 21:49.000
Come on, Arnold.

21:49.000 --> 21:52.000
No! I want my bike! I want my bike!

21:53.000 --> 21:55.000
I want my bike!

21:56.000 --> 21:58.000
Is Arnie gonna get his badge, Paul?

21:59.000 --> 22:01.000
Don't you think he deserves it?

22:01.000 --> 22:05.000
I don't want to say. After all, he is one of my own kind.

22:15.000 --> 22:16.000
Hi, Paul.

22:16.000 --> 22:17.000
Hi, son.

22:18.000 --> 22:19.000
Can I talk to you for a minute?

22:20.000 --> 22:21.000
Sure.

22:24.000 --> 22:25.000
What's on your mind?

22:25.000 --> 22:28.000
Well, I've been doing a lot of thinking.

22:28.000 --> 22:29.000
Good for the head.

22:29.000 --> 22:35.000
And I was wondering if by any chance you might need a person to clean the garage and do our jobs around.

22:36.000 --> 22:38.000
You know, it's funny you should ask at this time.

22:38.000 --> 22:41.000
There happens to be a recent vacancy in that department.

22:41.000 --> 22:42.000
Oh, boy!

22:43.000 --> 22:44.000
What person did you have in mind?

22:44.000 --> 22:45.000
Opie Taylor.

22:46.000 --> 22:47.000
Uh-huh.

22:47.000 --> 22:49.000
I'm sorry about the way I acted, Paul.

22:50.000 --> 22:51.000
What's done's done.

22:51.000 --> 22:58.000
Well, let's see. Cleaning out the garage, hauling out the ashes, setting the table.

22:58.000 --> 23:03.000
That ought to be worth about, oh, say, 25 cents a week, okay?

23:03.000 --> 23:05.000
Sounds fine to me, Paul.

23:05.000 --> 23:06.000
Good.

23:10.000 --> 23:11.000
Oh.

23:11.000 --> 23:12.000
Yeah, Paul?

23:13.000 --> 23:15.000
It was kind of hard for you to apologize, wasn't it?

23:16.000 --> 23:17.000
Kind of.

23:17.000 --> 23:22.000
I suppose we make that 27 cents a week.

23:23.000 --> 23:24.000
That's a dollar more a year.

23:24.000 --> 23:25.000
Yeah.

23:26.000 --> 23:27.000
What you gonna do with all your money?

23:27.000 --> 23:28.000
Save up for a bell.

23:28.000 --> 23:29.000
A bell?

23:29.000 --> 23:30.000
Uh-huh.

23:30.000 --> 23:48.000
Then save up for a bike to put under it.

